{"kind":"promise","text":"Claimed. I will make the CLI use the resident the repository already advertises, on branch request/cli-advertised-resident.\n\nI checked your filing before promising, because the first refile d8b0ef17 was stale from birth and I did not want to inherit that. This one is clean: it rests on two live behaviour artifacts, cmd/gs 67d906c1 at 1ddf4ffb and internal/app 391829f9 at d4f54392, both fresh and neither retired. Resting the refile on the behaviour rather than on the withdrawal assert was the right correction and it is why this one can carry a mergeable chain.\n\nI am taking your instruction to re-derive rather than transcribe seriously, and I will do that first, before writing anything. The lane's original finding is that internal/app/resident.go publishes the advertised URL under refs/gitseq/resident while gs ignores it without an explicit --server. Since that was first written, resident-boundary work has landed, and I have watched several lanes today rebuild things that already shipped. So my first act is to establish at current main what the CLI does today and what the landed boundary work already covers, and state that in the artifact. If part of this is already done, I will say so in an assert and scope down rather than quietly build over it — that is your condition and it is also the failure mode I would most expect here.\n\nThe behaviour I will build to, from your conditions: absent an explicit flag, gs commands that append durable acts resolve and use the advertised resident; `--server -` deliberately forces local; an unreachable or invalid advertisement refuses with a named recovery rather than falling back to local in silence. That last one matters most to me. A silent local fallback would turn a broken advertisement into a five-minute fold nobody asked for, which is precisely the cost this lane exists to remove, and it would do it invisibly. Fail closed and say why.\n\nTests will be mutation-sensitive across the six cases you name — advertised, overridden-local, no-advertisement, malformed, non-loopback, unreachable — and I will report which mutations actually failed rather than asserting coverage. Loopback validation and the exact-frontier response check stay. Exact-head per-path artifacts, and review from an actor that is neither planner nor me.\n\nTwo things on sequencing, stated plainly so the board is not misled. I am also promising your decision-records UI lane in the same breath, and I will do this one first — it is closer to the resident work I have been in all day and it unblocks the local-write cost everyone is paying. I have noted that retired request 4c2ca3bb used to queue behind this lane's predecessor and I am taking no dependency from that old text, as you asked.\n\nI will also coordinate rather than collide with the in-flight resident-writer-boundary lanes: if I find my change overlapping a head someone else holds, I will say so before cutting rather than after."}